\begin_layout Standard
An element of an image is usually associated to a square region centered on its coordinates.
 Its bounds are therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm0.5$
\end_inset

 around each dimension.
 The spatial calibration of the image can be used to relate index coordinates to physical space coordinates (see Section
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Spatial-Calibration"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Please be aware that spatial calibration stored in image files sometimes refers to the printing properties.
 It is therefore good practice to check the spatial calibration of images before performing any measurements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Three-dimensional images
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Most operators of the Image class can be applied indifferently on 2D or 3D images.
 For instance,
 computing the histogram of a 2D or 3D image relies on the same command.
 A script operating on 2D images can therefore easily by applied to a 3D image.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 3D images,
 several visualisation functions are presented in more details in section 
